WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.670 --> 00:00:03.040
AFIKA NYATI: Hi, my name is
Afika Nyati, and my research

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.070
concerns ways in which to
improve the digital reading

00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:07.690
experience.

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:10.270
I picked up on and came to
realize through my research

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:12.880
that part of the reason
why many people prefer

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.870
to read using print books as
opposed to digital alternatives

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:19.870
has to do with the fact
that many of the useful cues

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:22.090
present in print
books are lost when

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:24.700
you port over the
form and presentation

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.710
to digital context.

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:32.030
On top of this, many
people don't actually

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:34.400
find ways in which
to take the added

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.270
capabilities of digital
devices to improve the reading

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:40.670
experience over and
above what is possible

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:43.400
using print alternatives.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:48.480
My research concerned
combining the loss and these

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.440
added capabilities
to come up and devise

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.110
a new alternative
text presentation that

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:57.240
could aid digital reading.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.940
To inform the design
of this presentation,

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.560
I did a bit of
background research

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.260
on the visual and
cognitive processes

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.630
that happened during
reading, and came

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:08.980
to realize three things--

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:11.100
the first of which,
when we read,

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.380
we actually only read
within a specific region

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:17.470
within our visual system
called the FOVIO region.

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:19.680
And because we only can read
in this specific region,

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.060
we have to basically scan
the page to parse the words.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:27.150
And the scanning over time
introduces the cognitive load

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:30.690
associated with trying to
plan for the next gaze.

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:34.260
The second thing is, the
human brain can actually

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.950
process about four to
five information units.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:40.140
What this does is it sets
a constraint on the amount

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:43.740
of words you can actually
process at a specific given

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:45.400
moment.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:48.940
Lastly, knowing your
place within a document

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:52.270
is very important
in understanding

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:58.150
where you are, which gives you
a sense of the comprehension

00:01:58.150 --> 00:01:59.860
and building a
better understanding

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:02.350
of the text as a whole.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:04.180
So with this
information at hand,

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:07.090
we wanted to design
a presentation that

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:10.240
took that into account, but
also was benchmarked on three

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:13.840
metrics, namely, reading
speed, reading comprehension,

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:15.910
and workload index.

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:17.890
In an ideal world, we
wanted a presentation

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:21.190
that would allow the reader to
read at a rate much faster than

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:25.540
without it, however, not at
the expense of comprehension.

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:27.790
At the same time, we don't
want this presentation

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:31.245
to be a lot harder to
use, which would make it

00:02:31.245 --> 00:02:34.480
a lot harder for user adoption.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:37.290
In a nutshell, what the
presentation does is

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:40.470
it presents a set
fixed number of words

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:43.530
to the reader over
time, however,

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:48.290
at a rate that is dependent
on the words being presented.

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:50.600
To assess the performance
of his presentation,

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:53.420
we benchmarked it against
the standard reading

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:55.640
scroll-based text presentation.

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:57.140
We're currently
running the studies,

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:00.050
so there's no results
at the moment.

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:03.310
But we're optimistic
about the results.

